**Reused Evidence**
REUSE_JSON: {"evidence_ids":["E0001"],"lenses":["6B.1"]}
REUSE_JSON: {"evidence_ids":["E0003"],"lenses":["6B.1","6B.6","6B.8"]}

**New Evidence**
- Scholz told Ukraine donors that Germany, the Länder, and municipalities would ensure accommodation, medical care, school instruction, and a safe future for Ukrainian refugees, while also committing additional humanitarian aid. SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Rede von Bundeskanzler Olaf Scholz","publisher":"Bundesregierung","publication_date":"2022-04-11","url":"https://www.bundesregierung.de/breg-de/service/newsletter-und-abos/bulletin/rede-von-bundeskanzler-olaf-scholz-2025152","claim":"Scholz said the federal government, Länder, and municipalities would work together to provide Ukrainian refugees with accommodation, medical care, school instruction, and a safe future.","locator":"lines 175-197","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary government bulletin with direct speech by the chancellor and exact target-period wording.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Direct 2022 ruler-period evidence on crisis welfare response.","ruler_attribution":"Directly attributable to Scholz as chancellor; the commitment is government-wide rather than a personal administrative order.","contrary_evidence":["This is a pledge and crisis response statement, not proof of long-term outcomes by itself."],"lenses":["6B.1","6B.2","6B.3","6B.6","6B.8","6B.10"]}

- The cabinet/bill for child support was framed as helping children participate in society, education, training, and the labor market, with a one-off payment for adults on social minimum-security benefits. SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Sofortzuschlags- und Einmalzahlungsgesetz","publisher":"BMAS","publication_date":"2022-05-27","url":"https://www.bmas.de/DE/Service/Gesetze-und-Gesetzesvorhaben/sofortzuschlags-und-einmalzahlungsgesetz.html","claim":"The law says the child supplement is meant to improve children's participation in society, education, training, and the labor market before the Kindergrundsicherung starts, while adults on minimum-security benefits receive a one-off payment.","locator":"lines 792-804","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary ministry law page with explicit target, timing, and implementation status.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Direct 2022 social-policy enactment.","ruler_attribution":"Cabinet and parliamentary action under Scholz's government; implementation is institutional rather than personal.","contrary_evidence":["The measure is temporary and does not by itself establish durable structural improvement."],"lenses":["6B.2","6B.3","6B.8","6B.9","6B.10"]}

- The cabinet approved a child immediate supplement and one-off payment to support needy families and children under rising prices. SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Mehr Unterstützung für Familien mit wenig Geld","publisher":"BMAS","publication_date":"2022-03-16","url":"https://www.bmas.de/DE/Service/Presse/Pressemitteilungen/2022/mehr-unterstuetzung-fuer-familien-mit-wenig-geld.html","claim":"The cabinet approved a child immediate supplement of 20 euros per month and a 100 euro one-off payment for people receiving social minimum-security benefits.","locator":"lines 796-798","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary ministry press release stating the cabinet decision and beneficiary groups.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Direct 2022 decision and implementation timetable.","ruler_attribution":"Attributable to the Scholz cabinet through the responsible ministries; this is policy choice, not inherited baseline.","contrary_evidence":["It was an emergency measure during rising prices, not a comprehensive child-poverty solution."],"lenses":["6B.2","6B.3","6B.6","6B.8","6B.10"]}

- The Bundestag approved the government's bill to raise the statutory minimum wage to 12 euros per hour from 1 October 2022, benefiting more than six million workers, especially women and people in eastern Germany. SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Der Mindestlohn steigt auf 12 Euro","publisher":"BMAS","publication_date":"2022-06-03","url":"https://www.bmas.de/DE/Service/Presse/Meldungen/2022/mindestlohn-steigt-auf-12-euro.html","claim":"The Bundestag approved the federal government's bill to raise the statutory minimum wage to 12 euros per hour from 1 October 2022, benefiting over six million workers, especially women and people in eastern Germany.","locator":"lines 792-797","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary ministry release on a passed government bill with specific quantified beneficiaries.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Direct 2022 labor-market and household-income policy.","ruler_attribution":"A coalition policy implemented through Bundestag legislation under Scholz's government; not a personal administrative act.","contrary_evidence":["Inflation and high living costs still eroded real purchasing power in 2022."],"lenses":["6B.2","6B.3","6B.8","6B.10"]}

- Germany expanded housing support and heating relief, calling Wohngeld Plus the largest housing-benefit reform since the program began and saying it would support about 4.5 million people in two million households. SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Mehr Geld für die Wohnkosten im Winter","publisher":"BMWSB","publication_date":"2022-09-28","url":"https://www.bmwsb.bund.de/SharedDocs/pressemitteilungen/DE/2022/09/wohngeld-plus.html","claim":"The cabinet approved Wohngeld Plus and the second heating subsidy, described as the largest housing-benefit reform since the program began, supporting roughly 4.5 million people in two million households.","locator":"lines 277-279, 285","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary ministry press release with a concrete cabinet decision and beneficiary scale.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Direct 2022 crisis-response policy with 2023 implementation.","ruler_attribution":"Cabinet policy under Scholz; the measure is institutional and coalition-based rather than personal patronage.","contrary_evidence":["The main effect begins in 2023, so this is partly forward-looking rather than immediate 2022 delivery."],"lenses":["6B.2","6B.3","6B.6","6B.9","6B.10"]}

- Ukraine refugees were granted access from 1 June 2022 to basic-security benefits, with a single jobcenter point of contact, access to integration and language courses, labor-market support, childcare support, and health coverage. SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Couverture de base pour les réfugiés ukrainiens","publisher":"Gouvernement fédéral","publication_date":"2022-04-27","url":"https://www.bundesregierung.de/breg-fr/service/archives/couverture-de-base-ukrainiens-2028896","claim":"The federal government decided that registered Ukrainian refugees would receive extended basic-security benefits from 1 June 2022, with jobcenter-based access to integration and language courses, labor-market support, childcare support, and health coverage.","locator":"lines 141-171","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary government archive page with explicit cabinet decision and service details.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Direct 2022 refugee-protection policy.","ruler_attribution":"Federal cabinet action under Scholz; implementation shared with the Länder and local job centers.","contrary_evidence":["This is a policy-design document; it does not by itself prove long-run integration outcomes in 2022."],"lenses":["6B.2","6B.3","6B.6","6B.8","6B.10"]}

- Scholz said the government would provide more funding for refugee support and humanitarian aid, and that Ukrainian refugees were welcome and would be given accommodation, medical care, schooling, and a secure future. SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Rede von Bundeskanzler Olaf Scholz","publisher":"Bundesregierung","publication_date":"2022-04-11","url":"https://www.bundesregierung.de/breg-de/service/newsletter-und-abos/bulletin/rede-von-bundeskanzler-olaf-scholz-2025152","claim":"Scholz said Ukrainian refugees were welcome and that the government would ensure accommodation, medical care, schooling, and a secure future, alongside additional humanitarian funding.","locator":"lines 195-198","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary chancellor speech with direct welfare and refugee-protection commitments.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Direct 2022 target-period statement.","ruler_attribution":"Directly attributable to Scholz as chancellor.","contrary_evidence":["This is a commitment speech, not a delivery audit."],"lenses":["6B.1","6B.2","6B.6","6B.8","6B.10"]}

- The government stabilized Uniper and said it would not let anyone be overburdened, while adding further supports for households facing higher gas costs. SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Bundesregierung verständigt sich auf finanzielle Unterstützung für Uniper und kündigt weitere Entlastungen an","publisher":"Bundesministerium der Finanzen","publication_date":"2022-07-22","url":"https://www.bundesfinanzministerium.de/Content/DE/Pressemitteilungen/Finanzpolitik/2022/07/2022-07-22-finanzielle-unterstuetzung-fuer-uniper.html","claim":"The government approved a stabilization package for Uniper to secure gas and electricity supply, and Scholz said no one should be overburdened; the package also triggered further support measures for households and firms.","locator":"lines 257-280","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary joint government press release with direct chancellor quote and specific package details.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Direct 2022 crisis response during the energy shock.","ruler_attribution":"Government-level crisis management under Scholz; the action is collective but attributable to his cabinet leadership.","contrary_evidence":["The package is costly and partly aimed at preserving supply rather than redistributing gains."],"lenses":["6B.6","6B.9","6B.10"]}

- The IMF said Germany expanded income support for vulnerable households, cut fuel taxes, and provided liquidity support to firms; it also called the response timely and well-designed. SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"IMF Executive Board Concludes 2022 Article IV Consultation with Germany","publisher":"IMF","publication_date":"2022-07-20","url":"https://www.imf.org/en/news/articles/2022/07/19/pr22268-germany-imf-executive-board-concludes-2022-article-iv-consultation-with-germany","claim":"The IMF said the government was expanding income support for vulnerable households, cutting fuel taxes, and providing liquidity support to firms, and the Executive Board commended the authorities' timely and well-designed response to the Ukraine spillovers.","locator":"lines 243-258","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Independent multilateral assessment of the government's 2022 policy response with concrete policy details and evaluative language.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Direct assessment of 2022 policy in 2022.","ruler_attribution":"External appraisal of the Scholz government's response; not a personal evaluation of every implementing agency.","contrary_evidence":["The IMF also noted that some broad-based relief measures, such as fuel-tax cuts, were fiscally costly and distorted price signals."],"lenses":["6B.5","6B.6","6B.9","6B.10"]}

- The OECD said the government acted swiftly to support households and firms, and that the relief packages included social assistance, housing allowances, one-off transfers, permanent tax-indexation changes, and a housing-allowance reform, though some measures could be better targeted. SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"OECD Economic Surveys: Germany 2023","publisher":"OECD","publication_date":"2023","url":"https://www.oecd.org/en/publications/oecd-economic-surveys-germany-2023_9642a3f5-en/full-report/component-4.html","claim":"The OECD said the government took bold actions to support households and firms, with three relief packages and a 200 billion euro energy support fund that included social assistance, housing allowances, one-off transfers, and permanent policy changes such as an inflation adjustment of the tax schedule and reform of the housing allowance system.","locator":"lines 3928-3944","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Independent multilateral economic assessment with detailed package composition and policy critique.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Retroactive evaluation of 2022 policy response and 2023 reform design.","ruler_attribution":"External assessment of the Scholz government's policy choices; the government is credited for the package, not for every outcome effect.","contrary_evidence":["The OECD also said the measures were not well targeted at vulnerable households and highly exposed firms."],"lenses":["6B.5","6B.6","6B.9","6B.10"]}

- Destatis reported 7.9 percent annual inflation in 2022, driven mainly by energy and food price spikes after the war in Ukraine, while noting that relief measures such as the fuel discount, EEG surcharge abolition, and one-off gas/heating payment partially mitigated the rise. SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Inflation rate at +7.9% in 2022","publisher":"German Federal Statistical Office","publication_date":"2023-01-17","url":"https://www.destatis.de/EN/Press/2023/01/PE23_022_611.html","claim":"Germany's consumer prices rose 7.9 percent in 2022, mainly because of energy and food price spikes after the war in Ukraine; Destatis said relief measures partially mitigated the inflation rate.","locator":"press release text / annual-average section","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary official statistics release with exact annual figures and explanation.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Exact 2022 outcome data published immediately after the year ended.","ruler_attribution":"Outcome context under Scholz's government; inflation is not fully attributable to him, but policy response is part of the chapter record.","contrary_evidence":["The release also shows that household prices still surged sharply despite relief."],"lenses":["6B.6","6B.10"]}

- Destatis said employment in Germany in 2022 reached its highest level since reunification, with about 45.6 million people employed and 589,000 more than in 2021. SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Employment in 2022 at highest level since German unification","publisher":"German Federal Statistical Office","publication_date":"2023-01-02","url":"https://www.destatis.de/EN/Press/2023/01/PE23_001_13321.html","claim":"Average employment in 2022 reached about 45.6 million people, a record high since German unification, rising by 589,000 from the previous year.","locator":"lines 106-113","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary official statistics release with clear headline figures.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Exact 2022 outcome data.","ruler_attribution":"Macro outcome under Scholz's year in office, but heavily conditioned by preexisting labor-market strength and immigration.","contrary_evidence":["Employment strength does not offset the simultaneous erosion in real wages and persistent poverty risk."],"lenses":["6B.10","6B.2"]}

- Destatis revised real earnings in Germany down 4.0 percent in 2022 versus 2021. SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Real earnings, net earnings","publisher":"German Federal Statistical Office","publication_date":"2023-04-27","url":"https://www.destatis.de/EN/Themes/Labour/Earnings/Real-Earnings-Net-Earnings/_node.html","claim":"Revised results showed that real earnings in Germany decreased by an average 4.0 percent in 2022 compared with 2021.","locator":"press releases section, 27 April 2023 entry","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary official statistics summary with a revised annual estimate.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Exact 2022 outcome data, albeit published with revision in 2023.","ruler_attribution":"Outcome context rather than direct ruler control; useful as a constraint on welfare performance in Scholz's first full year.","contrary_evidence":["This is negative welfare evidence and shows that nominal gains were eroded by inflation."],"lenses":["6B.6","6B.10"]}

- Destatis said 20.9 percent of Germany's population was at risk of poverty or social exclusion in 2022, with the value nearly unchanged from 2021. SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Just over one-fifth of Germany’s population at risk of poverty or social exclusion","publisher":"German Federal Statistical Office","publication_date":"2023-05-16","url":"https://www.destatis.de/EN/Press/2023/05/PE23_190_63.html","claim":"Just over 17.3 million people in Germany were affected by poverty or social exclusion in 2022, equal to 20.9 percent of the population, and the value was almost unchanged from 2021.","locator":"lines 128-133","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary official statistics release with explicit annual poverty-risk figures and trend comparison.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Exact 2022 outcome data published in 2023.","ruler_attribution":"Outcome context under Scholz; this is not direct proof of causation, but it constrains claims of broad welfare improvement.","contrary_evidence":["The poverty-risk indicator did not meaningfully improve year on year, so the year was protective rather than transformative."],"lenses":["6B.8","6B.10"]}

**Search and Rejection Summary**
I considered roughly 30 documents across federal-government releases, BMAS/BMWSB/BMF material, Destatis releases, OECD, IMF, and later follow-up pages. I kept the strongest non-duplicative items above and rejected or deprioritized:
- Later 2024-2026 follow-up pages on refugee support, Grundsicherung, and energy policy because they were out of period or retrospective.
- Generic ministry landing pages and navigation-heavy pages that added no new ruler-period claim.
- Duplicate language versions of the same federal-government refugee release when they repeated the same substance.
- Macro-only pages that did not add a ruler-attributed social-welfare claim beyond the statistics already kept.
- Overlapping press materials that repeated the same relief-package facts without a materially new claim or locator.

**Remaining Gaps**
- Direct evidence on administrative competence inside the social-service bureaucracy is thin. I have policy outputs and external assessments, but not much on hiring, management quality, or implementation failures at the federal ministry level.
- Distributional evidence is incomplete for disability, migrant, and regional subgroups beyond the strong signals on women, eastern Germany, low-income families, and Ukrainian refugees.
- Durable institutional impact is only partly evidenced. Wohngeld Plus and the child supplement point in that direction, but long-run continuation beyond 2022 is not yet shown.
- If you want, I can do one more pass focused only on 6B.4, 6B.7, and 6B.9 to see whether there are stronger direct implementation or anti-patronage sources.
